## Edge cases

### Empty emphasis

Text with ** ** empty bold and * * empty italic (should render literally).

### Refs at boundaries

Start of paragraph: [[fact:arr]] is the ARR.

End of paragraph: the ARR is [[fact:arr]]

Ref alone: [[fact:nrr]]

### Multiple refs in one sentence

The company has $[[fact:arr]] ARR with [[fact:nrr]]% NRR, $[[fact:ltv]] LTV vs $[[fact:cac]] CAC giving [[term:ltv-to-cac]]x ratio and $[[fact:cash]] cash.

### Prefixed and suffixed refs

$[[fact:arr]] with dollar prefix, [[fact:nrr]]% with percent suffix, [[term:ltv-to-cac]]x with x suffix.

### Silent refs in prose

All checks pass[[~term:retention-check]] and metrics look healthy.

## Summary

$[[fact:arr]] ARR | [[fact:nrr]]% NRR | [[term:ltv-to-cac]]x LTV/CAC — **strong fundamentals** with $[[fact:cash]] runway.

## Aliased imports in refs

An alias valid in a code block must be valid in a prose ref — the alias here
is deliberately different from the module name (regression case for
issue #20, where alias-qualified refs silently degraded to plain text).

```scheme
;; pltg — alias ≠ module name
(import (quote ..facts.ai2ai_facts af))
```

Aliased ref: $[[fact:af.mrr]] MRR. Aliased with suffix: [[fact:af.yoy-growth-pct]]% YoY.
Module-qualified still works: $[[fact:ai2ai_facts.enterprise-arr]] enterprise ARR.
Adjacent aliased refs: [[fact:af.revenue]][[fact:af.cogs]] — comma-separated like any others.
Silent run collapses to one bracket: metrics hold[[~fact:af.mrr]][[~fact:af.revenue]][[~fact:af.cogs]] — three footnotes, one superscript.
